Run the SPIRE reconciler as a second pass inside innsegl reconcile (#153) (#155)

* fix(cmd): wire the SPIRE identity reconciler into reconcile's second pass (#153)

internal/spire.Reconciler had no caller outside its own tests, so the
identity drift it detects (spire_entry_missing, spire_entry_not_deleted,
spire_entry_duplicated, spire_entry_unattributed) ran nowhere in a
deployment. `innsegl reconcile` now runs it as a second pass alongside the
existing transparency-log reconciler, one command and one schedule as
decided, gated by -spire-address/-spire-server-id/-workload-api/-timeout
mirroring `reap`'s own flags exactly. The SPIRE dial itself is factored out
of reap.go into spiredial.go and shared by both subcommands rather than
duplicated.

The identity pass is off by default (existing deployments without SPIRE
configured are unaffected) and every cycle's report says explicitly when it
is off, in both text and JSON. Once -spire-address is set, a broken dial
fails reconcile closed rather than degrading silently, since a reconciler
missing one of its three halves is not a degraded reconciler.

deploy/compose/innsegl.yml documents the new flags but deliberately leaves
the pass off: server.conf's admin_ids authorizes exactly one identity,
spiffe://innsegl.dev/innsegl/mcp, and innsegl-reconciler's container cannot
attest as it. Turning this on needs a topology/threat-model decision (a
second admin identity, or reusing mcp's) this commit does not make.

